 "id": "2026-10-31-california-record-king-tides",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "California's coast just broke tide records. Why was the sea so high?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "At San Diego, water hit eight point five two feet above the average low-tide level on Tuesday, the National Weather Service says. That is the highest at that gauge on record. Santa Barbara, San Pedro and La Jolla also set record highs."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "The service blames a full moon on top of unusually warm water. Seas are about seventeen inches higher than last year, as heated water expands. Scripps pier measured seventy three point six degrees Wednesday, about nine above average."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Huntington Beach declared a local emergency. ABC Seven counted over one hundred damaged properties. Pacific Coast Highway closed, and Metrolink halted trains at San Clemente. The Los Angeles Times reports peak El Niño could add one to two feet more."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will California see even higher tides this winter? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
